      {
        "name": "Malt Extract",
        "fermentable_type": "extract",
        "folder_path": "Extract",
        "yield_fine_grind": {
          "unit": "%",
          "value": 80
        },
        "color": {
          "unit": "EBC",
          "value": 9.0
        },
        "recommend_mash": false,
        "notes": "Also known as: DME\n\nTypical color range: 4-79 EBC (typical 9)\n\nMalt Extract, available in dried (DME) or liquid (LME) form, has transformed the beer brewing process, making it accessible to more hobbyists. This is because the malt extract provides a convenient alternative to the traditional all-grain brewing method. Malt Extract is derived from malted grains, usually barley, where the sugars have been extracted and then either concentrated into a syrup (LME) or dehydrated into a powder (DME). This method eliminates the need for mashing, a time-consuming process used in all-grain brewing to extract fermentable sugars from malted grains.\n\nHowever, using malt extract over traditional grain malt can impact the final product. While malt extract can expedite the brewing process and often create a more consistent brew, all-grain brewing allows for greater control over the beer's flavor, color, and overall characteristics. Craft and professional brewers typically prefer the traditional grain malt method due to the increased scope for creativity and individuality it provides in beer creation. That said, malt extract brewing serves as a valuable entry point into the craft for newcomers, offering a less labor-intensive and less error-prone method while still producing quality beer."
      },
      {
        "name": "Acerola",
        "fermentable_type": "fruit",
        "folder_path": "Fruit",
        "yield_fine_grind": {
          "unit": "%",
          "value": 4
        },
        "color": {
          "unit": "EBC",
          "value": 23.0
        },
        "recommend_mash": false,
        "notes": "Acerola, a tropical fruit known for its high vitamin C content, has found an intriguing application in the world of beer brewing. The flavor profile of Acerola is described as tangy and slightly sweet with notes of cherry and apple. When used in beer brewing, it adds a refreshing acidity to the beverage, enhancing its overall taste. Acerola is primarily used as an adjunct or additive during fermentation to impart its unique flavors into the brew.\n\nApart from its distinct flavor contribution, Acerola also brings certain properties that benefit beer production. Its high vitamin C content acts as a natural antioxidant, helping to preserve the freshness and stability of the final product. Additionally, Acerola 's vibrant red color can add visual appeal to certain beer styles.\n\nTypically, brewers incorporate Acerola in lighter beer styles such as wheat beers or sour ales where its fruity acidity complements their delicate profiles. However, adventurous breweries have experimented with using Acerola in other types like IPAs or stouts to create unexpected flavor combinations and exciting twists on traditional recipes."
      },
      {
        "name": "Apple",
        "fermentable_type": "fruit",
        "folder_path": "Fruit",
        "yield_fine_grind": {
          "unit": "%",
          "value": 10
        },
        "color": {
          "unit": "EBC",
          "value": 7.0
        },
        "recommend_mash": false,
        "notes": "Typical color range: 0-49 EBC (typical 7)\n\nApple is a versatile ingredient that has found its way into the world of beer brewing. When used in brewing, it adds a distinct flavor profile to the finished product. The taste of Apple can range from sweet and tart to crisp and refreshing, depending on the variety used. It often imparts natural fruity notes with hints of acidity, creating a unique twist to traditional beer styles.\n\nThe use of Apples in brewing influences the taste of beer by adding complexity and depth to the overall flavor profile. This fruit provides a subtle sweetness that helps balance out bitterness while enhancing the aromatic qualities of certain hop varieties. Additionally, it contributes a pleasant tartness that can refresh the palate and make for an enjoyable drinking experience.\n\nApples are commonly used in various stages during beer production. They can be added as fresh fruit during fermentation or incorporated through juices, ciders, or even Apple concentrates during boiling or conditioning processes. With their high sugar content and natural fermentable properties, they contribute not only flavors but also sugars that yeast converts into alcohol.\n\nWhen brewed with Apples, different styles emerge showcasing this unique ingredient. Apple -infused beers such as cider-style brews highlight its flavors prominently while other styles like Belgian Witbier utilize Apples subtly alongside spices for added complexity. Some brewers experiment further by incorporating Apple into sour beers where its acidic nature complements wild yeasts' characteristics."
      },
      {
        "name": "Apricot",
        "fermentable_type": "fruit",
        "folder_path": "Fruit",
        "yield_fine_grind": {
          "unit": "%",
          "value": 9
        },
        "color": {
          "unit": "EBC",
          "value": 8.0
        },
        "recommend_mash": false,
        "notes": "Typical color range: 0-87 EBC (typical 8)\n\nApricot is a fruit that brings a unique flavor profile to beer brewing. Its sweet and slightly tart taste adds a refreshing twist to various beer styles. When used in brewing, apricot influences the taste of beer by imparting fruity notes and enhancing its overall complexity.\n\nApricot is commonly used in brewing for adding both aroma and flavor to beers. It can be utilized during different stages of the brewing process like fermentation or as an addition post-fermentation. This versatile fruit contributes a distinct character to many beer styles such as wheat beers, pale ales, sour beers, and Belgian-style brews.\n\nApart from its flavor-enhancing qualities, Apricot also possesses properties that make it suitable for use in beer brewing. It contains natural sugars which can ferment into alcohol during the fermentation process. Additionally, the high water content helps with maintaining hydration levels throughout the brewing process."
      },
      {
        "name": "Banana",
        "fermentable_type": "fruit",
        "folder_path": "Fruit",
        "yield_fine_grind": {
          "unit": "%",
          "value": 12
        },
        "color": {
          "unit": "EBC",
          "value": 1.0
        },
        "recommend_mash": false,
        "notes": "Typical color range: 0-15 EBC (typical 1)\n\nBanana is a versatile fruit that has found its way into the world of beer brewing, adding unique flavors and characteristics to various styles. Its flavor profile is distinctively sweet, with notes of ripe banana and sometimes even hints of tropical fruits like pineapple or mango. When used in beer brewing, banana can greatly influence the taste by imparting a fruity sweetness and contributing to the overall complexity.\n\nThe use of Bananas in beer brewing serves several purposes. It can enhance the aroma and flavor profile, creating a more enjoyable drinking experience for consumers. Additionally, it can contribute to the mouthfeel of the beer by adding body and smoothness. Banana 's properties as an ingredient include high sugar content, which provides fermentable sugars for yeast during fermentation. This helps create alcohol while leaving behind pleasant fruity esters.\n\nTypical beer styles brewed with Bananas include Hefeweizen (a German wheat ale), Dunkelweizen (a darker version of Hefeweizen), and certain Belgian-style beers such as Witbier or Saison . These styles often showcase banana flavors alongside other complementary ingredients like cloves or spices, resulting in refreshing and flavorful brews that are loved by many craft beer enthusiasts worldwide."
      },
      {
        "name": "Beets",
        "fermentable_type": "fruit",
        "folder_path": "Fruit",
        "yield_fine_grind": {
          "unit": "%",
          "value": 7
        },
        "color": {
          "unit": "EBC",
          "value": 55.0
        },
        "recommend_mash": false,
        "notes": "Typical color range: 3-399 EBC (typical 55)\n\nBeets, a versatile and vibrant fruit, have found an interesting application in beer brewing. With their earthy and slightly sweet flavor profile, Beets add a unique twist to the taste of beer. They bring in subtle notes of vegetal sweetness that can range from mild to robust depending on the quantity used.\n\nWhen incorporated into beer recipes, Beets influence the taste by adding color as well as enhancing its overall complexity. The natural sugars present in Beets ferment during the brewing process, contributing to both alcohol content and providing a touch of sweetness. Additionally, beet's earthiness complements various styles like stouts or porters by imparting rich flavors that pair well with roasted malts.\n\nBrewers often use Beets for their coloring properties due to their deep red hue. This natural dye allows them to create visually striking beers without resorting to artificial additives. Beetroots are also valued for their health benefits as they contain essential nutrients such as vitamins A and C along with antioxidants known for promoting heart health.\n\nTypical beer styles brewed with Beets include Beet Saison or Red Ale where they shine through prominently alongside other ingredients like spices or fruits. These brews showcase not only the aesthetic appeal but also highlight how beet's distinct flavor elevates traditional beer profiles into something more adventurous and memorable"
      },
      {
        "name": "Blackberry",
        "fermentable_type": "fruit",
        "folder_path": "Fruit",
        "yield_fine_grind": {
          "unit": "%",
          "value": 5
        },
        "color": {
          "unit": "EBC",
          "value": 212.0
        },
        "recommend_mash": false,
        "notes": "Typical color range: 0-687 EBC (typical 212)\n\nBlackberry is a versatile fruit that has found its way into the world of beer brewing, adding unique flavor profiles and enhancing the taste of various beer styles. Its distinct sweet-tart taste and aroma contribute to creating a delightful balance in beers. The inclusion of Blackberries in brewing can influence the taste by imparting fruity notes, subtle acidity, and sometimes even a hint of earthiness.\n\nBlackberries are often used in brewing for their ability to add complexity and depth to different beer styles such as pale ales, wheat beers, sours, or even stouts. Due to their vibrant color and rich flavors, they lend themselves well to fruit-forward or tart creations. Blackberry -infused beers can range from light-bodied with refreshing tanginess to full-bodied brews with pronounced berry characteristics. Blackberries bring natural sugars that ferment during the brewing process while also contributing antioxidants and vitamins. They provide brewers with an opportunity to experiment with flavors and create exciting new combinations that cater to diverse palates."
      },
      {
        "name": "Blood Orange",
        "fermentable_type": "fruit",
        "folder_path": "Fruit",
        "yield_fine_grind": {
          "unit": "%",
          "value": 9
        },
        "color": {
          "unit": "EBC",
          "value": 18.0
        },
        "recommend_mash": false,
        "notes": "Typical color range: 1-93 EBC (typical 18)\n\nBlood Orange is a unique fruit that imparts a distinct flavor profile to beer brewing. Known for its vibrant crimson color and sweet-tart taste, Blood Orange s bring a citrusy and slightly bitter note to the beer. The flesh of the fruit has hints of raspberry, strawberry, and even pomegranate, which adds complexity to the brew. This infusion of flavors contributes to an overall refreshing and zesty experience.\n\nWhen used in beer brewing, Blood Orange greatly influences the taste by adding a burst of citrusy freshness. It enhances the aroma while balancing out sweetness with its natural acidity. Additionally, it brings some bitterness that complements hoppy beers or balances out sweeter malt profiles.\n\nThe application of Blood Orange in beer brewing can be versatile. It can be used in various styles such as IPAs ( India Pale Ales ), wheat beers, sour beers, and Belgian-style brews. Its properties include high levels of Vitamin C and antioxidants along with distinctive pigments derived from anthocyanins found in their peel."
      },
      {
        "name": "Blueberry",
        "fermentable_type": "fruit",
        "folder_path": "Fruit",
        "yield_fine_grind": {
          "unit": "%",
          "value": 10
        },
        "color": {
          "unit": "EBC",
          "value": 12.0
        },
        "recommend_mash": false,
        "notes": "Typical color range: 0-392 EBC (typical 12)\n\nBlueberry, a delightful fruit packed with antioxidants and unique flavor, has found its way into the realm of craft beer brewing, adding an unexpected twist to a variety of traditional beer styles. Typically, the most common beer styles brewed with blueberries are fruit and vegetable beers, wheat ales, sours, and certain types of stouts. These styles are chosen because they possess flavor profiles that naturally complement the sweet, slightly tart characteristics of the blueberry.\n\nThe use of blueberries in beer brewing infuses the beer with a distinct fruity flavor and aroma that can vary based on the amount and form of blueberries used (fresh, frozen, pureed, or juice). When blueberries are fermented in beer, they yield a subtly sweet and tart flavor, while the fragrance takes on a fresh, fruity, and slightly earthy dimension. Depending on the brewing process, the finished product can range from mildly fruity to robustly berry-like, with color hues varying from light purplish to deep, dark blue. Such distinctive characteristics make blueberry-infused beers a popular choice among craft beer enthusiasts who crave innovation and unique taste experiences."
      },
      {
        "name": "Carambola",
        "fermentable_type": "fruit",
        "folder_path": "Fruit",
        "yield_fine_grind": {
          "unit": "%",
          "value": 4
        },
        "color": {
          "unit": "EBC",
          "value": 23.0
        },
        "recommend_mash": false,
        "notes": "Carambola, also known as starfruit, is a tropical fruit that has found its way into beer brewing due to its unique flavor profile. The fruit has a crisp and juicy texture with a tangy-sweet taste reminiscent of citrus fruits like pineapple and lime. When added to beer during the brewing process, Carambola brings an exotic twist to the taste by imparting hints of tropical flavors.\n\nThe addition of Carambola in beer not only influences the taste but also adds complexity and depth to the final product. Its tartness helps balance out any sweetness from malt or other fruits used in brewing, resulting in a well-rounded flavor profile. Carambola can be used for various purposes in beer production, including adding subtle fruity aromas or serving as the main flavor component.\n\nOne of the key properties of Carambola is its high acidity level which makes it suitable for fermentation processes. This fruit contains natural enzymes that aid in breaking down sugars into alcohol during fermentation, contributing to the overall character of the beer. While Carambola can be incorporated into different styles of beers such as wheat beers or pale ales, it is particularly popular in sour beers where its tanginess complements and enhances their refreshing nature."
      },
      {
        "name": "Carrot",
        "fermentable_type": "fruit",
        "folder_path": "Fruit",
        "yield_fine_grind": {
          "unit": "%",
          "value": 5
        },
        "color": {
          "unit": "EBC",
          "value": 12.0
        },
        "recommend_mash": false,
        "notes": "Typical color range: 0-52 EBC (typical 12)\n\nCarrots are a root vegetable often employed in innovative and experimental brewing practices. While not traditionally a staple in beer brewing, carrots are becoming more popular in craft beers, especially in styles such as Gose, Saison, or Vegetable beers. They add a unique character, especially in beers brewed for autumnal harvest celebrations or as distinctive seasonal offerings.\n\nWhen used in brewing, carrots lend a subtle sweetness and a distinct earthiness to the beer's flavor profile. The vegetable’s naturally occurring sugars can also contribute to the fermentation process, potentially influencing the beer's alcohol content. Additionally, when used in combination with particular yeast strains and brewing techniques, carrots can impart a vibrant, often orange-like hue to the beer, making these brews visually intriguing as well. However, the use of carrots should be well-balanced, as excessive amounts can overwhelm the beer's taste and make it more vegetable-forward than desired."
      },
      {
        "name": "Cashew",
        "fermentable_type": "other",
        "folder_path": "Fruit",
        "yield_fine_grind": {
          "unit": "%",
          "value": 6
        },
        "color": {
          "unit": "EBC",
          "value": 7.0
        },
        "recommend_mash": false,
        "notes": "Cashews, although known for their use in culinary dishes and snacks, have also found a unique application in the world of beer brewing. When used as an ingredient, Cashew imparts a distinct flavor profile to the brew. The rich and creamy taste of Cashews adds a smooth nuttiness that complements various beer styles.\n\nInfluencing the taste of beer, Cashews enhance its overall complexity and provide a subtle sweetness. This versatile ingredient is commonly utilized during different stages of brewing such as mashing or fermentation. Apart from adding flavor, Cashews also contribute to the mouthfeel and body of the beer. Cashew 's properties make it suitable for multiple purposes within brewing. It can be employed directly by incorporating roasted or raw nuts into the mash or used indirectly through extracts or oils derived from Cashews . Moreover, due to its high oil content, careful consideration must be given to prevent excessive lipids in finished beers brewed with this ingredient.\n\nTypical beer styles that incorporate Cashew include stouts, porters, brown ales and even some experimental IPAs . These styles benefit greatly from the addition of Cashew as it enhances their richness while adding depth to their flavors without overpowering other ingredients present in the recipe."
      },
      {
        "name": "Cherry",
        "fermentable_type": "fruit",
        "folder_path": "Fruit",
        "yield_fine_grind": {
          "unit": "%",
          "value": 12
        },
        "color": {
          "unit": "EBC",
          "value": 12.0
        },
        "recommend_mash": false,
        "notes": "Typical color range: 4-132 EBC (typical 12)\n\nCherry is a versatile fruit that adds a unique and delicious flavor profile to beer. Known for its sweet and tart taste, cherry brings a delightful fruity quality to the brew. It influences the taste of beer by providing a distinct aroma and enhancing the overall complexity of flavors. The natural sweetness of cherries can balance out bitterness in hop-forward beers or add an extra layer of depth to lighter, more delicate styles.\n\nCherries are commonly used in brewing as either whole fruits, juice, or puree forms. They can be added during fermentation or aging processes to allow their flavors to infuse into the beer. Cherries also bring some properties that make them ideal for brewing such as their vibrant red color which lends itself well to visually appealing beers. Additionally, they contain antioxidants that contribute health benefits. Typically, cherry is used in various beer styles including sour ales like kriek and Flanders red ale where it complements the acidity with its natural tartness. Cherry wheat beers are another popular style where the fruit's sweetness pairs harmoniously with wheat malt characteristics."
      },
      {
        "name": "Clementine",
        "fermentable_type": "fruit",
        "folder_path": "Fruit",
        "yield_fine_grind": {
          "unit": "%",
          "value": 9
        },
        "color": {
          "unit": "EBC",
          "value": 12.0
