        "color": {
          "unit": "EBC",
          "value": 159.0
        },
        "recommend_mash": false,
        "notes": "Typical color range: 0-732 EBC (typical 159)\n\nCandi Sugar is a type of sugar that is commonly used in beer brewing to add complexity and depth of flavor. It has a rich, caramel-like flavor profile with notes of molasses and dark fruits. When added to the brewing process, it can influence the taste of beer by increasing sweetness and providing subtle flavors that enhance the overall character.\n\nIn addition to its flavor-enhancing properties, Candi Sugar also serves several practical purposes in beer brewing. It acts as a source of fermentable sugars for yeast during fermentation, which can help increase alcohol content and improve body and mouthfeel. Furthermore, Candi Sugar can contribute to color development in beers, adding deep amber or dark brown hues depending on the type used.\n\nCandi Sugar finds application across various beer styles, but it is particularly common in Belgian-style beers such as Dubbels, Tripels, and Quadrupels. These styles often feature complex malt profiles with high alcohol content, making them well-suited for the use of Candi Sugar to achieve desired characteristics like increased sweetness and richness."
      },
      {
        "name": "Candi Syrup",
        "fermentable_type": "sugar",
        "folder_path": "Sugar",
        "yield_fine_grind": {
          "unit": "%",
          "value": 70
        },
        "color": {
          "unit": "EBC",
          "value": 212.0
        },
        "recommend_mash": false,
        "notes": "Typical color range: 1-480 EBC (typical 212)\n\nCandi Syrup is a popular ingredient in beer brewing due to its unique flavor profile. It adds rich, complex notes of caramel, toffee, and dark fruit to the beer, enhancing its overall taste. The syrup's deep flavors greatly influence the final product by adding sweetness and depth that can complement or balance other ingredients in the recipe.\n\nIn addition to providing flavor, Candi Syrup also serves various purposes in brewing. It acts as a fermentable sugar source for yeast during fermentation, contributing towards increased alcohol content and carbonation. Furthermore, it aids in improving body and mouthfeel while promoting better head retention.\n\nCandi Syrup possesses specific properties that make it an ideal choice for certain beer styles. Its high concentration of sugars allows brewers to achieve higher gravity levels without using excessive malt quantities. This makes it particularly suitable for stronger beers such as Belgian Dubbels or Tripels where a fuller body and higher ABV are desired. Additionally, Candi Syrup can be used creatively in other styles like stouts or barleywines to add complexity and depth of flavor."
      },
      {
        "name": "Cane Sugar",
        "fermentable_type": "sugar",
        "folder_path": "Sugar",
        "yield_fine_grind": {
          "unit": "%",
          "value": 100
        },
        "color": {
          "unit": "EBC",
          "value": 20.0
        },
        "recommend_mash": false,
        "notes": "Typical color range: 0-20 EBC (typical 20)\n\nCane Sugar is commonly used in beer brewing for its ability to enhance the flavor profile of the brew. It imparts a sweet and slightly caramelized taste to the beer, adding depth and complexity to its overall flavor. This sweetness can help balance out bitter or hoppy flavors, resulting in a more well-rounded brew.\n\nIn addition to enhancing the taste of beer, Cane Sugar also serves as a fermentable ingredient that provides extra fuel for yeast during fermentation. As yeast consumes the sugars present in Cane Sugar, it produces alcohol and carbon dioxide, contributing to both the ABV (alcohol by volume) and carbonation levels of the finished beer.\n\nCane Sugar is typically used in lighter-bodied beers such as pale ales, lagers, pilsners, and certain Belgian styles like tripels and saisons . Its properties include being highly fermentable and easily dissolved into wort (unfermented beer), making it convenient for brewers to use during various stages of brewing."
      },
      {
        "name": "Caramel",
        "fermentable_type": "sugar",
        "folder_path": "Sugar",
        "yield_fine_grind": {
          "unit": "%",
          "value": 90
        },
        "color": {
          "unit": "EBC",
          "value": 119.0
        },
        "recommend_mash": false,
        "notes": "Typical color range: 5-600 EBC (typical 119)\n\nCaramel is a widely used ingredient in beer brewing that adds a sweet and toasty flavor profile to the final product. It influences the taste of beer by imparting rich, caramelized notes that can range from light and subtle to dark and intense, depending on the degree of caramelization. This addition brings depth and complexity to the overall flavor profile, creating a more balanced and enjoyable drinking experience.\n\nIn brewing, caramel is primarily used for its ability to enhance both color and aroma while adding sweetness. Its properties include providing body and mouthfeel, improving head retention, stabilizing foam structure, and contributing natural preservative properties. Caramel malts are commonly added during mashing or steeping in order to extract their flavors into the wort before fermentation begins. Caramel is typically utilized in various beer styles such as amber ale, brown ale, bock beers (both traditional German bocks like Maibock or Doppelbock ), Scottish ales (including Scotch Ale or Wee Heavy), Märzen/Oktoberfest lagers, Irish red ale, Belgian dubbel/tripel/quadrupel styles etc., where it plays an integral role in defining their characteristic taste profiles."
      },
      {
        "name": "Corn Sugar",
        "fermentable_type": "sugar",
        "folder_path": "Sugar",
        "yield_fine_grind": {
          "unit": "%",
          "value": 91
        },
        "color": {
          "unit": "EBC",
          "value": 0.0
        },
        "recommend_mash": false,
        "notes": "Also known as: Dextrose\n\nTypical color range: 0-1 EBC (typical 0)\n\nCorn Sugar, also known as dextrose or corn syrup solids, is a fermentable sugar commonly used in beer brewing. It has a subtle and clean flavor profile that adds sweetness without imparting any distinct taste of its own. When added to the brewing process, Corn Sugar increases the alcohol content of beer while lightening its body and mouthfeel.\n\nIn addition to boosting alcohol levels, Corn Sugar is often used for priming during bottle conditioning. This involves adding a small amount of sugar to fermented beer before bottling, which creates carbonation through secondary fermentation. Corn Sugar 's high fermentability makes it an ideal choice for this purpose as it quickly converts into CO2 without leaving residual sweetness behind.\n\nThe properties of Corn Sugar include its solubility in water, rapid fermentation rate by yeast, and ability to contribute lighter flavors and textures to beers. Its use is particularly common in American lagers such as Budweiser or Coors where it helps create crispness and dryness but can also be found in various other styles like cream ales or blonde ales that benefit from increased drinkability and enhanced clarity provided by Corn Sugar s' characteristics."
      },
      {
        "name": "Corn Syrup",
        "fermentable_type": "sugar",
        "folder_path": "Sugar",
        "yield_fine_grind": {
          "unit": "%",
          "value": 78
        },
        "color": {
          "unit": "EBC",
          "value": 1.0
        },
        "recommend_mash": false,
        "notes": "Also known as: Brewers Crystals\n\nTypical color range: 0-4 EBC (typical 1)\n\nCorn Syrup is widely used in beer brewing as a fermentable sugar. It has a mild and neutral flavor profile, which makes it an ideal ingredient for enhancing the taste of various beer styles. Its use can contribute to the overall sweetness and body of the beer without imparting any distinct flavors.\n\nIn terms of its influence on the taste of beer, Corn Syrup helps to balance out bitterness by adding a touch of sweetness. It also contributes to the mouthfeel and texture, giving beers a smoother and fuller body. Additionally, Corn Syrup aids in fermentation as yeast easily consumes its sugars during the brewing process.\n\nThe properties of Corn Syrup make it highly desirable for brewers. Not only does it have excellent fermentability, but it also adds stability to beers by preventing excessive thinning or drying out during fermentation. Corn Syrup is commonly used in light lagers and American-style adjunct lagers due to its ability to produce crisp and refreshing brews with well-rounded flavors."
      },
      {
        "name": "Honey",
        "fermentable_type": "honey",
        "folder_path": "Sugar",
        "yield_fine_grind": {
          "unit": "%",
          "value": 76
        },
        "color": {
          "unit": "EBC",
          "value": 4.0
        },
        "recommend_mash": false,
        "notes": "Typical color range: 1-79 EBC (typical 4)\n\nHoney is widely used in beer brewing for its distinctive flavor profile and its ability to enhance the taste of different beer styles. The unique floral, fruity, and sometimes earthy notes of honey can add complexity and sweetness to a variety of brews. Its influence on the taste of beer depends on several factors such as the type and origin of the honey used, as well as how it is incorporated into the brewing process.\n\nIn addition to providing flavor, honey also serves multiple purposes in beer production. It acts as a fermentable sugar source for yeast during fermentation, contributing to alcohol content in the final product. Honey 's natural antimicrobial properties make it an excellent preservative that prolongs shelf life while maintaining freshness. Moreover, its sticky texture helps improve body and mouthfeel in beers.\n\nDifferent types of beers can be brewed with honey depending on desired flavors and characteristics. Varieties like mead (honey wine) exclusively use honey as their primary fermentable sugar source while other traditional styles incorporate it alongside malted barley or other grains. Popular examples include braggot (a combination of mead and ale), Belgian strong ales like Tripels or Saisons which often feature honey-like sweetness, or even experimental craft brews where brewers explore unique combinations using various hop profiles along with specific types of honeys such as orange blossom or clover varieties."
      },
      {
        "name": "Lactose (Milk Sugar)",
        "fermentable_type": "sugar",
        "folder_path": "Sugar",
        "yield_fine_grind": {
          "unit": "%",
          "value": 93
        },
        "color": {
          "unit": "EBC",
          "value": 1.0
        },
        "recommend_mash": false,
        "fermentable_fraction": {
          "unit": "%",
          "value": 0
        },
        "notes": "Typical color range: 0-1 EBC (typical 1)\n\nLactose, also known as milk sugar, is a commonly used ingredient in beer brewing. It adds a distinct flavor profile to the finished product with its subtle sweetness and creamy texture. Lactose is unfermentable by yeast, meaning it remains in the final beer without being converted into alcohol during fermentation. This not only enhances the body and mouthfeel of the beer but also provides a smooth and velvety finish.\n\nLactose contributes a mild sweetness that balances out bitterness from hops or roasted malts in certain styles of beer. It is often used to create sweeter and richer brews like milk stouts or cream ales . The addition of Lactose can give these beers an added complexity and depth while enhancing their overall drinkability.\n\nAside from influencing taste, Lactose has unique properties that make it useful in brewing. Its unfermentable nature means it can be added at various stages of the brewing process without affecting primary fermentation or carbonation levels. Brewers typically add Lactose during boiling or post-fermentation to achieve desired sweetness levels."
      },
