          "value": 10
        },
        "notes": "Typical color range: 0-7 EBC (typical 6)\n\nMaltodextrin, a sugar derived from starch, is commonly used in beer brewing as a way to enhance body and mouthfeel. It has a neutral flavor profile, meaning it does not add any noticeable taste or aroma to the beer. Instead, Maltodextrin primarily influences the texture of the beer by increasing its viscosity and providing a fuller mouthfeel.\n\nIn addition to improving body and mouthfeel, Maltodextrin serves various purposes in beer brewing. It can be used to increase the alcohol content without adding excessive sweetness or flavor. Maltodextrin also aids in head retention by providing proteins that stabilize foam formation. Furthermore, it acts as an adjunct ingredient that helps balance out bitter flavors in hop-forward beers.\n\nThe properties of Maltodextrin make it highly suitable for use in different styles of beer. Its ability to boost body and improve mouthfeel makes it particularly popular in heavier styles such as stouts and porters where richness is desired. However, it can also be utilized in lighter styles like pale ales or lagers when brewers want to add substance without altering the overall character too much."
      },
      {
        "name": "Maple Syrup",
        "fermentable_type": "sugar",
        "folder_path": "Sugar",
        "yield_fine_grind": {
          "unit": "%",
          "value": 66
        },
        "color": {
          "unit": "EBC",
          "value": 92.0
        },
        "recommend_mash": false,
        "notes": "Typical color range: 69-92 EBC (typical 92)\n\nMaple Syrup is a versatile ingredient that adds a unique flavor profile to beer brewing. It imparts a rich, sweet taste with hints of caramel and toffee, enhancing the complexity of the brew. The distinct flavors from the syrup can range from subtle undertones in lighter beers to more prominent notes in darker and heavier styles.\n\nInfluencing both aroma and taste, Maple Syrup is primarily used as an adjunct or flavoring agent in beer production. Its natural sweetness helps balance out bitterness while adding depth and richness to the overall flavor profile. The properties of Maple Syrup also contribute to improved mouthfeel, giving the beer a smooth and velvety texture.\n\nBeer styles that are typically brewed with Maple Syrup include stouts, porters, brown ales, barleywines, and even some Belgian-style beers. These styles benefit from its robust sweetness and complement it well with their malty backbone. As a sugar source during fermentation or added post-fermentation for enhanced character, Maple Syrup brings an enticing twist to these already complex brews."
      },
      {
        "name": "Molasses",
        "fermentable_type": "sugar",
        "folder_path": "Sugar",
        "yield_fine_grind": {
          "unit": "%",
          "value": 75
        },
        "color": {
          "unit": "EBC",
          "value": 212.0
        },
        "recommend_mash": false,
        "notes": "Typical color range: 80-212 EBC (typical 212)\n\nMolasses, a thick and viscous byproduct of sugar production, is widely used in beer brewing due to its rich flavor profile. It imparts a distinct deep caramel and earthy taste that adds complexity to the overall flavor profile of the beer. The use of Molasses can greatly influence the taste of beer, giving it a sweet and robust character.\n\nAside from its flavor contribution, Molasses also serves as a fermentable source of sugars for yeast during fermentation. This helps increase the alcohol content in the final product while adding depth to the body and mouthfeel. Its properties include being highly fermentable yet offering residual sweetness, contributing both color and aroma to the brew.\n\nMolasses finds application in various styles of beer such as stouts, porters, brown ales, and Belgian dubbels among others. These styles benefit from its bittersweet notes that complement roasted malts or add complexity alongside spices like cinnamon or nutmeg commonly found in winter warmers or holiday beers. With its unique flavor profile and versatile usage potential, Molasses continues to be an essential ingredient for brewers looking to craft bold and flavorful beers."
      },
      {
        "name": "Rice Syrup",
        "fermentable_type": "sugar",
        "folder_path": "Sugar",
        "yield_fine_grind": {
          "unit": "%",
          "value": 78
        },
        "color": {
          "unit": "EBC",
          "value": 1.0
        },
        "recommend_mash": false,
        "notes": "Typical color range: 1-4 EBC (typical 1)\n\nRice Syrup, a sugar derived from rice, has found its application in beer brewing due to its unique flavor profile and properties. It imparts a light and crisp sweetness to the beer, enhancing the overall taste and mouthfeel. The use of Rice Syrup in brewing can result in a drier finish and lighter body compared to other sugars like malt or corn syrup.\n\nRice Syrup is primarily used as an adjunct ingredient by brewers for various purposes. It can serve as a fermentable sugar source during the brewing process, providing additional alcohol content without adding excess flavors or colors. Rice Syrup also aids in improving head retention and promoting a clean fermentation profile. Due to its light flavor characteristics, Rice Syrup is commonly employed in brewing styles where subtlety is key. It is often used in lagers such as American Light Lager and Japanese Rice Lager, contributing to their refreshing nature while maintaining balance with other ingredients."
      },
      {
        "name": "Sorghum Syrup",
        "fermentable_type": "sugar",
        "folder_path": "Sugar",
        "yield_fine_grind": {
          "unit": "%",
          "value": 76
        },
        "color": {
          "unit": "EBC",
          "value": 3.0
        },
        "recommend_mash": false,
        "notes": "Typical color range: 1-52 EBC (typical 3)\n\nSorghum syrup is a natural sweetener that has been used in beer brewing for centuries. It boasts a unique flavor profile, with hints of molasses, caramel, and honey-like sweetness. When added to beer recipes, it imparts a distinct taste that can range from subtle to pronounced depending on the amount used. The addition of Sorghum syrup influences the taste of beer by providing depth and complexity to its flavor profile. Its rich sweetness balances out bitterness and adds a pleasant touch of sweetness without overwhelming other flavors. This makes it an excellent choice for brewers looking to create well-rounded beers with a hint of sweetness.\n\nSorghum syrup serves various purposes in brewing. It is commonly employed as an alternative source of fermentable sugars for gluten-free or gluten-reduced beers since it does not contain gluten like barley malt does. Additionally, Sorghum syrup can be utilized as a flavor enhancer or adjunct ingredient in traditional beers to augment their overall taste experience. It has high fermentability which allows yeast to easily convert its sugars into alcohol during fermentation. It also contributes color and body to the finished beer due to its natural viscosity and dark hue.\n\nTypical beer styles brewed with Sorghum syrup include gluten-free lagers and pale ales specifically designed for individuals with celiac disease or those seeking alternatives to traditional barley-based brews. These styles showcase the ability of Sorghum syrup to mimic some characteristics found in conventional malts while maintaining their own distinct flavors derived from this unique sugar source."
      },
      {
        "name": "Sugar",
        "fermentable_type": "sugar",
        "folder_path": "Sugar",
        "yield_fine_grind": {
          "unit": "%",
          "value": 100
        },
        "color": {
          "unit": "EBC",
          "value": 1.0
        },
        "recommend_mash": false,
        "notes": "Typical color range: 0-92 EBC (typical 1)\n\nSugar is used in a variety of beer styles, each with its own unique reasons for its inclusion. In traditional Belgian ales like Tripels and Quads, sugars are added to increase the alcohol content without adding body, keeping the beer light and drinkable. Belgian candi sugar, in particular, is favored for its contribution to the complex fruity and spicy flavors characteristic of these styles. On the other hand, in British ales, brewers often add invert sugar, which can lend a rounded sweetness and improve head retention.\n\nThe flavor profile influenced by sugar largely depends on the type used and the style of beer. In general, using sugar in brewing can result in a drier, less filling beer with increased alcoholic content. However, certain specialty sugars can also impart specific flavor nuances. For instance, dark candi sugar or brown sugar can contribute caramel or toffee notes, while honey or agave can add a subtle floral or fruity sweetness. Sugar usage is therefore a valuable tool in a brewer's arsenal, allowing them to fine-tune the balance of flavors in their creations."
      },
      {
        "name": "Turbinado",
        "fermentable_type": "sugar",
        "folder_path": "Sugar",
        "yield_fine_grind": {
          "unit": "%",
          "value": 99
        },
        "color": {
          "unit": "EBC",
          "value": 25.0
        },
        "recommend_mash": false,
        "notes": "Typical color range: 20-25 EBC (typical 25)\n\nTurbinado, a type of sugar commonly used in beer brewing, brings its distinct flavor profile and influences the taste of the final product. With its rich caramel-like flavor and subtle molasses undertones, Turbinado adds depth and complexity to beers. Its unique sweetness complements various styles by providing a smooth mouthfeel and enhancing overall balance.\n\nApart from imparting flavors, Turbinado is primarily used as a fermentable sugar during the brewing process. It serves as an additional food source for yeast during fermentation, resulting in increased alcohol content without adding excessive sweetness or heaviness to the finished beer. Due to its high solubility rate, it easily dissolves into wort while also contributing desirable color characteristics.\n\nThe properties of Turbinado make it suitable for different beer styles such as stouts, porters, Belgian ales, brown ales, and even some IPAs . Its ability to lend complexity without overpowering other ingredients allows brewers to create well-rounded brews with added depth. Whether highlighting roasted malts in dark beers or complementing fruity esters in Belgian-style ales – Turbinado has become an essential ingredient for craft brewers seeking unique flavor profiles in their creations."
      }
