        },
        "beta_acid_range": {
          "minimum": {
            "unit": "%",
            "value": 2.2
          },
          "maximum": {
            "unit": "%",
            "value": 5.9
          }
        },
        "notes": "Also known as: Mighty Axe Julius\n\nFlavor notes: Orange, Peach, Sweet\n\nJulius hops have their origins in Minnesota, where they were cultivated at Mighty Axe Hops. The unique combination of Minnesota's soil composition and climate, along with Mighty Axe Hops' specialized harvesting techniques, has led to the development of Julius as a distinct variety of Cascade hops.\n\nThe flavor profile of Julius hops is characterized by its powerful notes of citrus, tropical fruit, and pine, which make it an exceptional choice for a wide range of beer styles, particularly IPAs and Pale Ales. The hops impart a bright, juicy character to the brew, with hints of orange, mango, and passion fruit, alongside a subtle yet assertive resinous quality. Homebrewers appreciate the versatility of Julius hops for both bittering and late aroma additions, allowing them to experiment with different hopping schedules to achieve the desired balance between bitterness and aroma. When used in the brewing process, Julius hops contribute not only to the flavor but also to the overall mouthfeel, providing a smooth and pleasant drinking experience. So, if you're looking to brew a vibrant, fruit-forward beer with a touch of piney bitterness, Julius hops are definitely worth exploring."
      },
      {
        "name": "Kazbek",
        "hop_type": "aroma",
        "origin": "Czech Republic",
        "folder_path": "Czech Republic",
        "alpha_acid": {
          "unit": "%",
          "value": 6.4
        },
        "alpha_acid_range": {
          "minimum": {
            "unit": "%",
            "value": 4.1
          },
          "maximum": {
            "unit": "%",
            "value": 8.0
          }
        },
        "beta_acid": {
          "unit": "%",
          "value": 5.0
        },
        "beta_acid_range": {
          "minimum": {
            "unit": "%",
            "value": 2.0
          },
          "maximum": {
            "unit": "%",
            "value": 6.0
          }
        },
        "substitutes": "Saaz, Sterling",
        "notes": "Flavor notes: Berries, Citrus, Grapefruit, Lemon, Lime, Noble, Passion Fruit, Peach, Pineapple, Spicy\n\nKazbek hops, developed in the Czech Republic, are a product of a cross between a wild Russian variety and the daughter of Saaz and Northern Brewer . This cross was made in 1984, but Kazbek was officially released only in 2008. The hop is named after one of the highest mountains in the Caucasus Range, reflecting its wild Russian heritage.\n\nKazbek is often referred to as a \"Super Saaz\" due to its amplified aroma profile compared to its parent, Saaz. It offers a unique blend of citrus, sweet fruits, and berries, while retaining the classic noble spice. The predominant aromas include grapefruit, lemon, and lime, accompanied by sweet fruits such as pineapple, passion fruit, peach, and watermelon. Light top notes of honey and cream caramel are also present. These aromas also occur in the raw hops where they are joined by nuances of raspberries, orange, ginger, elderberries, and chocolate. Light notes of lemon balm add particular emphasis to the overall aroma."
      },
      {
        "name": "Keyworth's Early",
        "hop_type": "aroma",
        "origin": "Great Britain",
        "folder_path": "Great Britain",
        "alpha_acid": {
          "unit": "%",
          "value": 7.4
        },
        "alpha_acid_range": {
          "minimum": {
            "unit": "%",
            "value": 6.0
          },
          "maximum": {
            "unit": "%",
            "value": 7.9
          }
        },
        "beta_acid": {
          "unit": "%",
          "value": 2.0
        },
        "beta_acid_range": {
          "minimum": {
            "unit": "%",
            "value": 2.0
          },
          "maximum": {
            "unit": "%",
            "value": 2.0
          }
        },
        "notes": "Flavor notes: Aromatic, Bright, Citrus, Fruity, Grapefruit, Lemon, Pungent, Refreshing, Tangy, Zesty\n\nKeyworth's Early hops are originating from Wye College in England during the 1920s, they were developed from a wild New Mexico male in open pollination. Selected in 1943, Keyworth's Early was one of the few hop varieties that showed moderate resistance to wilt disease, a significant concern for hop growers at the time. Despite its initial popularity due to this resistance, it fell out of favor due to low yields and was discontinued for a period. However, the variety has experienced a revival thanks to the modern palate's appreciation for its flavor characteristics.\n\nTalking about flavor, Keyworth's Early is renowned for its pungent grapefruit and lemon notes, which can impart a refreshing citrus character to beers. This makes it particularly suitable for American Ales, where its moderate to high alpha acid composition and the unique combination of myrcene and humulene oils contribute both to the beer's aroma and its bittering qualities. The hop's lemon and grapefruit notes are complemented by a pungent flavor that is sometimes likened to 'Manitoban' characteristics."
      },
      {
        "name": "Klon 18",
        "hop_type": "aroma",
        "origin": "Ukraine",
        "folder_path": "Ukraine",
        "alpha_acid": {
          "unit": "%",
