      {
        "name": "Riwaka",
        "hop_type": "aroma",
        "origin": "New Zealand",
        "folder_path": "New Zealand",
        "alpha_acid": {
          "unit": "%",
          "value": 5.5
        },
        "alpha_acid_range": {
          "minimum": {
            "unit": "%",
            "value": 4.1
          },
          "maximum": {
            "unit": "%",
            "value": 7.0
          }
        },
        "beta_acid": {
          "unit": "%",
          "value": 4.5
        },
        "beta_acid_range": {
          "minimum": {
            "unit": "%",
            "value": 2.6
          },
          "maximum": {
            "unit": "%",
            "value": 4.5
          }
        },
        "substitutes": "Calypso, Centennial, Citra, Saaz",
        "notes": "Flavor notes: Citrus, Grapefruit, Passion Fruit, Tropical Fruits\n\nRiwaka hops, also known as D Saaz, are a unique aroma hop variety originating from New Zealand. They were bred during the development of New Zealand Hops' 'hops with a difference' programme, and were officially released to the brewing world in 1997. The breeding process involved crossing the \"Old Line\" Saaz with specially developed New Zealand breeding selections. Riwaka hops are known for their abundant oil content, which is nearly twice that of its Saazer parent, contributing to its distinct flavor profile.\n\nRiwaka hops are characterized by their extreme citrus notes, with aromas of passionfruit, grapefruit, lemon, and other tropical fruits. This powerful grapefruit and citrusy character is literally breathtaking during selection, making Riwaka a standout variety. The pure weight of the oil character experienced during selection carries right through to the glass, making it a punchy addition to new world styles of Pale Ales and New Zealand Pilseners. It's also suitable for big styles such as IPAs and Imperial Pilsners. Some brewers even consider Riwaka the \"ultimate\" hop for hoppy beers due to its strong sweet citrus note. If you're a homebrewer looking to say \"New Zealand Hops\" in your beer, Riwaka is an excellent choice."
      },
      {
        "name": "Saaz",
        "hop_type": "aroma",
        "origin": "Czech Republic",
        "folder_path": "Czech Republic",
        "alpha_acid": {
          "unit": "%",
          "value": 3.5
        },
        "alpha_acid_range": {
          "minimum": {
            "unit": "%",
            "value": 2.4
          },
          "maximum": {
            "unit": "%",
            "value": 5.8
          }
        },
        "beta_acid": {
          "unit": "%",
          "value": 3.7
        },
        "beta_acid_range": {
          "minimum": {
            "unit": "%",
            "value": 2.0
          },
          "maximum": {
            "unit": "%",
            "value": 4.1
          }
        },
        "substitutes": "Lubelska, Sorachi Ace, Sterling, Tettnanger",
        "notes": "Flavor notes: Earthy, Floral, Herbal, Mild, Noble, Spicy\n\nSaaz hops, a noble variety with a rich history, have long been celebrated for their distinct characteristics and contribution to the world of beer brewing. Hailing from the Czech Republic, these hops trace their roots back to the town of Žatec (Saaz in German), which has cultivated the esteemed hop since the 11th century. Saaz hops have earned their noble status due to their unique flavor profile, consistent performance, and limited availability, making them a sought-after choice for brewers worldwide.\n\nThe flavor profile of Saaz hops is delicate and complex, characterized by a pleasant earthiness with subtle floral and spicy notes. These qualities impart a refined, elegant aroma to the beer, making Saaz hops a favorite among homebrewers and professional brewers alike. The hops' relatively low alpha acid content, typically ranging between 2.5% and 4.5%, means that they primarily contribute aroma and flavor rather than significant bitterness. Consequently, Saaz hops shine in traditional lager styles, most notably in Czech Pilsners and Bohemian Pilsners, where their trademark flavors meld seamlessly with the clean, crisp malt profile.\n\nFor homebrewers looking to experiment with Saaz hops, it is essential to consider their unique qualities and how they will impact the final beer. As a noble hop, Saaz is best utilized in styles that emphasize balance and subtlety. Adding Saaz hops during the late stages of the boil or as a dry hop can maximize their aromatic contributions while minimizing any harsh bitterness. When used appropriately, Saaz hops can elevate your homebrew to new heights, bringing centuries of brewing tradition and a touch of European elegance to your creations."
      },
      {
        "name": "Sabro",
        "hop_type": "aroma",
        "origin": "United States",
        "folder_path": "United States",
        "alpha_acid": {
