          },
          "maximum": {
            "unit": "%",
            "value": 4.0
          }
        },
        "notes": "Mistral is coming from the legendary growing region of Alsace, France and was released in 2019 by the French hop breeding program. It is a dual-purpose hop, meaning it can be used for both bittering and aroma. Mistral is known for its complex and intense aroma profile, which includes notes of tropical fruit, citrus, herbal, and floral. Its alpha acid content ranges from 5% to 10%, making it suitable for use in a wide range of beer styles.\n\nMistral hops are often used in hop-forward beers such as IPAs, pale ales, and Belgian-style ales. Its high oil content makes it an excellent choice for dry hopping, as it can contribute significant aroma and flavor to the beer. In addition to its aromatic properties, Mistral also provides a clean and smooth bitterness, making it a versatile hop for use in a variety of beer recipes. Overall, Mistral is a promising hop variety that is gaining popularity among brewers for its unique aroma profile and versatility in beer production."
      },
      {
        "name": "Mosaic",
        "hop_type": "aroma/bittering",
        "origin": "United States",
        "folder_path": "United States",
        "alpha_acid": {
          "unit": "%",
          "value": 12.4
        },
        "alpha_acid_range": {
          "minimum": {
            "unit": "%",
            "value": 10.6
          },
          "maximum": {
            "unit": "%",
            "value": 21.3
          }
        },
        "beta_acid": {
          "unit": "%",
          "value": 3.5
        },
        "beta_acid_range": {
          "minimum": {
            "unit": "%",
            "value": 2.0
          },
          "maximum": {
            "unit": "%",
            "value": 4.0
          }
        },
        "substitutes": "Citra, Simcoe",
        "notes": "Also known as: HBC 369\n\nFlavor notes: Berries, Blueberry, Bubblegum, Citrus, Earthy, Floral, Grapefruit, Grassy, Papaya, Pine, Rose, Spicy, Stone Fruit, Tangerine, Tropical Fruits\n\nMosaic hops, a relatively recent addition to the world of brewing, have quickly become a favorite among homebrewers and craft breweries alike. Developed by the Hop Breeding Company in 2012, Mosaic is a unique hybrid of the Nugget and Simcoe hop varieties, which are both well-regarded for their distinct flavor profiles. The name \" Mosaic \" was chosen to reflect the complexity and versatility of this hop, offering a stunning array of flavors and aromas that can be used to create truly memorable beers.\n\nThe Mosaic hop is known for its complex flavor profile, which includes notes of tropical fruit, citrus, pine, and earthy undertones. The fruity flavors, reminiscent of mango, pineapple, and tangerine, are often the most prominent, making Mosaic an ideal choice for hop-forward beers such as IPAs, pale ales, and New England IPAs . The high alpha acid content (typically between 11-14%) provides ample bittering potential, while the high concentration of essential oils contributes to its distinct and powerful aroma. This combination of characteristics allows homebrewers to experiment with Mosaic hops in single-hop brews or as a part of a more diverse hop blend, adding depth and nuance to their creations.\n\nWhen using Mosaic hops in your homebrew, consider adding them at various stages of the brewing process to achieve the desired balance of bitterness, flavor, and aroma. For example, adding Mosaic hops early in the boil will provide a solid bittering backbone, while later additions will contribute more to the hop flavor and aroma. You can also experiment with dry hopping to further enhance the tropical and citrus notes of Mosaic hops. As a versatile and highly sought-after hop variety, Mosaic is an excellent choice for homebrewers looking to craft beers with exceptional flavor and aroma profiles."
      },
      {
        "name": "Most",
        "hop_type": "aroma/bittering",
        "origin": "Czech Republic",
        "folder_path": "Czech Republic",
        "alpha_acid": {
