          },
          "maximum": {
            "unit": "%",
            "value": 9.0
          }
        },
        "notes": "Also known as: Brooklyn\n\nFlavor notes: Earthy, Grapefruit, Hay, Passion Fruit, Pine, Tropical Fruits\n\nMoutere, previously known as experimental hop HORT 0605, is a relatively new hop variety that was first introduced in 2015 by New Zealand's hop breeding program under the name \"Brooklyn\", but later renamed. It was named after the Moutere Hills region in the northern part of New Zealand's South Island, where it was first cultivated. Moutere hops are known for their intense tropical fruit aroma and flavor, with notes of passionfruit, guava, and citrus.\n\nMoutere hops have a high oil content, which contributes to their unique flavor profile. They also have a high alpha acid content, making them a versatile hop that can be used for both bittering and aroma. Moutere hops are typically used in pale ales, IPAs, and other hop-forward beer styles. Brewers appreciate the complexity and depth of flavor that Moutere hops bring to their beers, and they are becoming increasingly popular among craft breweries around the world."
      },
      {
        "name": "National",
        "hop_type": "aroma/bittering",
        "origin": "Ukraine",
        "folder_path": "Ukraine",
        "alpha_acid": {
          "unit": "%",
          "value": 6.3
        },
        "alpha_acid_range": {
          "minimum": {
            "unit": "%",
            "value": 5.5
          },
          "maximum": {
            "unit": "%",
            "value": 8.2
          }
        },
        "notes": "Also known as: Natsionalnyi\n\nFlavor notes: Balanced, Clean, Delicate, Earthy, Grassy, Green Tea, Herbal, Soft, Spicy\n\nNational hops, also known as Natsionalnyi, are a Ukrainian variety with a unique heritage tied to the country's hop-growing tradition. They are generally considered a dual-purpose hop, though often categorized as an aroma hop thanks to their distinct bitter-aromatic profile. With an alpha acid content of around 5.5%, they offer a balance between smooth bitterness and nuanced aromatics, making them a versatile choice for homebrewers exploring both ales and lagers.\n\nIn terms of flavor and aroma, National hops provide a soft grassy character complemented by delicate notes of green tea. This gives beers brewed with them a layered depth that leans toward herbal and slightly earthy tones, while still remaining clean and balanced. Their moderate bitterness and refined aromatic qualities make them well-suited for a wide range of styles, from Light Golden Ales and lagers to more robust dark ales, where they can contribute a grounding hop presence without overwhelming the malt character.\n\nHomebrewers seeking alternatives to more common varieties like Magnum, Nugget, or Northern Brewer may find National hops a compelling option, especially when looking to add a subtle herbal complexity with a uniquely Ukrainian origin. They can serve as a reliable base bittering hop or as a late addition to showcase their nuanced aromatics."
      },
      {
        "name": "Nectaron",
        "hop_type": "aroma/bittering",
        "origin": "New Zealand",
