      {
        "name": "Ginger Root (1)",
        "local_id": "Misc_93",
        "folder_path": "Canadian Homebrewers Association",
        "misc_type": "herb",
        "use_for": "Holiday Beer",
        "notes": "Adds distinctive ginger flavor for Ginger Ales, Holiday Beers.  Fresh grated ginger root from the grocer is best.  As little as 1/2 ounce per 5 gallons is noticable, but up to 4 oz can be used for ginger ales."
      },
      {
        "name": "Yeast Lightning",
        "local_id": "Misc_94",
        "folder_path": "Canadian Homebrewers Association",
        "misc_type": "other"
      },
      {
        "name": "Irish Moss (1)",
        "local_id": "Misc_95",
        "folder_path": "Canadian Homebrewers Association",
        "misc_type": "fining",
        "use_for": "Clarity",
        "notes": "Fining agent that aids in the post-boil protein break.  Reduces protein chill haze and improves beer clarity."
      },
      //
      // We don't (currently) support color in Misc ingredients, but it's in the source material so I have left it
      // commented out here in case we want to add support for it at a future date.
      //
      {
        "name": "Birch Bark",
        "misc_type": "other",
        "folder_path": "Other",
//        "color": {
//          "unit": "EBC",
//          "value": 12.0
//        },
        "notes": "Birch Bark, a spice and herb used in beer brewing, adds a unique flavor profile to the final product. With its distinct earthy and slightly sweet taste, Birch Bark influences the taste of beer by imparting subtle notes of wintergreen, mint, and even leather. This natural ingredient is often used for its aromatic qualities and ability to enhance complexity in various beer styles.\n\nApart from adding flavor, Birch Bark has several properties that make it beneficial for brewing. It contains compounds such as betulinic acid and methyl salicylate which contribute to its characteristic aroma and potential health benefits. Additionally, the antimicrobial properties found in Birch Bark can aid in preserving the freshness of beers during fermentation.\n\nTypically utilized in craft breweries or homebrewing settings, Birch Bark finds application in different beer styles including stouts/porters, saisons/farmhouse ales, and gruits (herb-infused beers). Its addition can add depth to dark beers like stouts by complementing their roasty flavors with hints of herbal sweetness. In saisons or farmhouse ales where wild yeast strains are frequently employed for fermentation, Birch Bark contributes an intriguing layer of complexity alongside other spices like coriander or black pepper. Gruits benefit from this botanical ingredient as well because it imparts an earthy quality that aligns with their historical use of herbs instead of hops as bittering agents."
      },
      {
        "name": "Chai",
        "misc_type": "other",
        "folder_path": "Other",
//        "color": {
//          "unit": "EBC",
//          "value": 40.0
//        },
        "notes": "Typical color range: 3-77 EBC (typical 40)\n\nChai, a blend of spices and herbs commonly used in tea, has found its way into the world of beer brewing. With its distinct flavor profile that includes notes of cinnamon, cardamom, ginger, cloves, and black pepper, Chai brings a warm and spicy complexity to beers. It adds depth and richness to the taste by influencing both the aroma and flavor profiles.\n\nChai is primarily used as an adjunct ingredient during the brewing process to enhance certain styles of beer. Its properties contribute flavors that pair well with malty or robust beer styles such as stouts, porters, brown ales, or even winter warmers. The combination of Chai 's spiciness with these darker beers creates a harmonious balance that often results in a comforting brew ideal for cooler seasons or festive occasions. Chai -infused beers can have varying levels of spice intensity depending on brewers' preferences but they typically offer an inviting warmth with layers of complex flavors from each individual spice component within Chai itself."
      },
      {
        "name": "Chili",
        "misc_type": "other",
        "folder_path": "Other",
//        "color": {
//          "unit": "EBC",
//          "value": 95.0
//        },
        "notes": "Chili, a popular spice and herb, has found its way into the world of beer brewing, adding a unique twist to traditional brews. Known for its fiery heat and distinct flavor profile, Chili brings intense spiciness and subtle smokiness to beers. Its influence on taste can vary depending on the quantity used - from providing just a hint of warmth in lighter styles to delivering an explosive kick in stronger ones.\n\nThe addition of Chili to beer is primarily used for enhancing flavors and creating new sensory experiences. It adds complexity by introducing spicy notes that complement or contrast with other ingredients like hops or malt sweetness. Chili 's properties go beyond just heat; it also contributes aromas such as earthy undertones or fruity characteristics when added during fermentation. This versatile ingredient finds its place in various beer styles, including stouts, IPAs ( India Pale Ales ), lagers, and even sour beers – each offering a different balance between heat and flavor intensity."
      },
      {
        "name": "Chipotle Pepper",
        "misc_type": "other",
        "folder_path": "Other",
//        "color": {
//          "unit": "EBC",
//          "value": 47.0
//        },
        "notes": "Typical color range: 4-47 EBC (typical 47)\n\nChipotle Pepper, a smoked and dried jalapeño pepper, has found its way into the world of beer brewing to add an intriguing twist to traditional brews. Known for its smoky flavor profile with hints of earthiness and spiciness, Chipotle Pepper brings a unique aroma and taste that can elevate any beer recipe. When used in moderation, it imparts a subtle smokiness that harmonizes well with various malt flavors.\n\nThe addition of Chipotle Pepper greatly influences the taste of beer by adding complexity and depth to the overall flavor profile. It introduces a gentle heat that lingers on the palate without overpowering other ingredients. Brewers often use this ingredient during different stages of the brewing process such as mashing or boiling to extract desired characteristics from it.\n\nThis spice is commonly used in crafting robust beers like stouts, porters, brown ales, or even Belgian-style saisons where it complements rich chocolatey notes or balances out sweeter malts with its mild spiciness. With their versatile properties, brewers are able to experiment creatively while incorporating Chipotle Peppers into their recipes to create truly unique and flavorful craft beers."
      },
      {
        "name": "Cinnamon",
        "misc_type": "spice",
        "folder_path": "Spice",
//        "color": {
//          "unit": "EBC",
//          "value": 7.0
//        },
        "notes": "Typical color range: 0-252 EBC (typical 7)\n\nCinnamon, a popular spice derived from the bark of certain tree species, has found its way into the world of beer brewing. Known for its warm and sweet flavor profile with hints of spiciness, cinnamon adds depth and complexity to the taste of beer. Its distinct aroma and taste are easily recognizable and can enhance various beer styles.\n\nWhen used in brewing, cinnamon influences the taste of beer by imparting its unique characteristics. It brings a pleasant warmth to the brew while adding subtle notes of sweetness and spice. Cinnamon is often combined with other ingredients like vanilla or nutmeg to create complex flavors that complement different beer styles.\n\nCinnamon is primarily used as an adjunct ingredient in brewing to add additional layers of flavor to beers such as stouts, porters, winter ales, or pumpkin ales. It pairs well with malty beers due to its ability to balance out their richness with its own distinctive taste. The properties of cinnamon make it suitable for both mashing during brewing or added directly during fermentation stages."
      },
      {
        "name": "Clove",
        "misc_type": "spice",
        "folder_path": "Spice",
//        "color": {
//          "unit": "EBC",
//          "value": 12.0
//        },
        "notes": "Typical color range: 1-12 EBC (typical 12)\n\nClove is a popular spice used in beer brewing, known for its distinct flavor profile that includes warm and spicy notes. Its strong aroma adds complexity to the taste of beer, influencing it with hints of clove and sometimes even a slight sweetness. The use of clove in brewing enhances the overall sensory experience by providing an additional layer of flavor.\n\nCloves are commonly used as an ingredient in various beer styles such as Belgian witbiers, hefeweizens, and certain spiced ales. It is often added during different stages of the brewing process like mashing or boiling to impart its unique flavors into the brew. Cloves also possess antimicrobial properties that can help preserve beer and prevent spoilage."
      },
      {
        "name": "Coconut",
        "misc_type": "other",
        "folder_path": "Other",
//        "color": {
//          "unit": "EBC",
//          "value": 15.0
//        },
        "notes": "Typical color range: 0-134 EBC (typical 15)\n\nCoconut is a versatile ingredient that adds a unique flavor profile to beer brewing. Its tropical and nutty taste, along with its creamy texture, can greatly influence the overall taste of the beer. When used in moderation, coconut imparts a subtle sweetness and an exotic aroma that complements various beer styles.\n\nCoconut is commonly used for adding depth and complexity to stouts, porters, and brown ales . It enhances the richness of these darker beers by imparting flavors reminiscent of toasted coconut flakes or even coconut milk. Additionally, it can be used in lighter styles such as wheat beers or IPAs to provide a refreshing twist.\n\nThe properties of coconut make it ideal for brewing due to its high oil content which contributes to both mouthfeel and head retention. Coconut also contains fermentable sugars that add some additional sweetness during fermentation. Moreover, brewers often use shredded coconut or extract derived from coconuts when incorporating this ingredient into their recipes."
      },
      {
        "name": "Coffee Beans",
        "misc_type": "other",
        "folder_path": "Other",
//        "color": {
//          "unit": "EBC",
//          "value": 202.0
//        },
        "notes": "Typical color range: 32-269 EBC (typical 202)\n\nCoffee Beans are a popular ingredient in beer brewing, adding unique flavors and aromas to the final product. The flavor profile of Coffee Beans can vary greatly depending on factors such as roasting level and origin, but common notes include roasted, chocolatey, nutty, and even fruity undertones. When used in brewing, Coffee Beans infuse their distinct flavors into the beer, creating a rich and complex taste.\n\nThe properties of Coffee Beans contribute not only to flavor but also aroma in beer brewing. As they are brewed alongside malted grains during fermentation or cold-brewed separately before blending into the base beer, their oils release aromatic compounds that create enticing scents reminiscent of freshly brewed coffee. They add depth and complexity to the malt character while imparting a pleasant bitterness that balances out sweetness. Coffee bean-infused beers often have a smooth mouthfeel with hints of cocoa or dark chocolate that enhance the overall drinking experience.\n\nCoffee Beans are primarily used in specialty beers like stouts and porters due to their complementary flavors. These styles typically feature robust malts with roasted characteristics that pair well with the natural bitterness of Coffee Beans . Additionally, some brewers experiment with incorporating coffee into lighter styles such as pale ales or IPAs for added complexity."
      },
      {
        "name": "Coffee",
        "misc_type": "other",
        "folder_path": "Other",
//        "color": {
//          "unit": "EBC",
//          "value": 439.0
//        },
        "notes": "Typical color range: 64-871 EBC (typical 439)\n\nCoffee has become a popular addition to beer brewing, adding depth and complexity to the flavor profile. Its rich and robust taste brings notes of roasted bitterness, chocolatey sweetness, and even hints of fruitiness. The use of coffee in beer can greatly influence its taste by enhancing malt flavors or balancing out hop bitterness. It provides an earthy undertone that complements various beer styles.\n\nCoffee is primarily used as an ingredient during the brewing process, either added directly into the mash or as part of the cold brew extraction method. Its properties include caffeine content which adds a slight kick to the final product while also contributing antioxidants that offer potential health benefits. Coffee -infused beers are commonly found in stouts and porters due to their natural compatibility with dark malts and roasted flavors, but it can also be incorporated into other styles such as brown ales or IPAs for a unique twist on traditional recipes."
      },
      {
        "name": "Coriander",
        "misc_type": "other",
        "folder_path": "Other",
//        "color": {
//          "unit": "EBC",
//          "value": 1.0
//        },
        "notes": "Typical color range: 1-19 EBC (typical 1)\n\nCoriander, a popular spice and herb, is widely used in beer brewing to add unique flavors and aromas. It has a distinct flavor profile characterized by citrusy notes with hints of lemon and orange zest. When added to the brewing process, coriander can greatly influence the taste of beer by providing a refreshing and zesty character.\n\nDue to its versatile nature, coriander is used for various purposes in beer production. It acts as a natural preservative that helps extend the shelf life of beers while also enhancing their overall flavor. Additionally, it contributes to the complexity of certain styles such as Belgian Witbier or German Hefeweizen. The properties of coriander include its ability to balance sweetness with subtle bitterness and provide an aromatic quality that complements other ingredients in the brew. Typical beer styles brewed with coriander include witbiers (Belgian wheat beers), saisons (farmhouse ales), gose (sour German-style beers), and some spiced winter warmers."
      },
      {
        "name": "Fennel",
        "misc_type": "other",
        "folder_path": "Other",
//        "color": {
//          "unit": "EBC",
//          "value": 6.0
//        },
        "notes": "Typical color range: 0-12 EBC (typical 6)\n\nFennel, a versatile spice and herb, has found its application in the world of beer brewing. Known for its distinct flavor profile that is described as earthy, sweet, and slightly licorice-like, Fennel brings a unique taste to beers. Its influence on the taste of beer can range from subtle hints to more pronounced flavors depending on the amount used during brewing.\n\nFennel can be employed for various purposes. It serves as an excellent ingredient for adding complexity and depth to both traditional and experimental brews. Fennel 's properties include being rich in essential oils such as anethole which contributes to its characteristic aroma and flavor. Additionally, it possesses natural preservative qualities that help prolong the shelf life of brewed beers. Typically used in Belgian-style or spiced beers like saisons or witbiers where herbal flavors are appreciated, Fennel adds an intriguing twist by providing hints of licorice alongside other spices present in these styles."
      },
      {
        "name": "Ginger",
        "misc_type": "spice",
        "folder_path": "Spice",
//        "color": {
//          "unit": "EBC",
//          "value": 4.0
//        },
        "notes": "Typical color range: 0-128 EBC (typical 4)\n\nGinger is a versatile spice that has found its way into the world of beer brewing, adding a unique flavor profile and complexity to various brews. With its distinct spicy and slightly sweet taste, Ginger brings a refreshing kick to beers. Its influence on the taste depends on how it's used - whether grated, sliced, or juiced - but generally, it lends a zesty and warming quality to the final product.\n\nGinger is widely used in brewing as both an ingredient during fermentation and as an addition post-fermentation for additional aroma and flavor enhancement. It can be added at different stages of the brewing process to achieve varying intensities of Ginger character in beer. Additionally, Ginger provides natural preservative properties due to its antimicrobial compounds.\n\nGinger pairs well with various beer styles such as wheat beers (e.g., Belgian witbier ), pale ales, IPAs ( India Pale Ales ), stouts, and even sour beers. Whether it's complementing fruity esters in lighter styles or balancing hop bitterness in more robust brews like IPAs or stouts, Ginger adds depth and complexity while maintaining its own distinctive identity within the overall flavor profile of these beers."
      },
      {
        "name": "Green Tea",
        "misc_type": "other",
        "folder_path": "Other",
//        "color": {
//          "unit": "EBC",
//          "value": 31.0
//        },
        "notes": "Typical color range: 10-51 EBC (typical 31)\n\nGreen tea has found its way into the world of beer brewing, adding a unique flavor profile and enhancing the taste of various brews. Its distinctive grassy and slightly bitter notes bring a refreshing twist to traditional beer styles. The use of Green tea in brewing influences the taste by imparting earthy flavors along with hints of citrus and floral undertones.\n\nApart from flavor enhancement, Green tea is used for its numerous properties that benefit both brewers and consumers alike. It acts as a natural preservative due to its antibacterial qualities, extending shelf life while maintaining freshness. Additionally, Green tea contains antioxidants that contribute to potential health benefits when consumed in moderation.\n\nTypical beer styles brewed with Green tea range from pale ales and wheat beers to experimental or specialty brews like IPA-infused teas. This infusion offers an exciting fusion between two popular beverages – combining the aromas and flavors of hops with the distinct characteristics of Green tea spices & herbs"
      },
      {
        "name": "Hazelnut",
        "misc_type": "other",
        "folder_path": "Other",
//        "color": {
//          "unit": "EBC",
//          "value": 12.0
//        },
        "notes": "Typical color range: 12-55 EBC (typical 12)\n\nHazelnut is a popular ingredient in beer brewing due to its distinct flavor profile. It adds a rich and nutty taste to the beer, enhancing its overall complexity. The influence of Hazelnut on the taste of beer can vary depending on how it is used during the brewing process. When added as whole roasted nuts or crushed kernels, Hazelnut provides a pronounced aroma and flavor that complements certain styles of beer.\n\nThe application of Hazelnut in beer brewing extends beyond just adding flavor. It also contributes to the mouthfeel and body of the brew, giving it a smooth and velvety texture. Hazelnuts are commonly used in stouts, porters, brown ales, and other dark beers where their earthy notes blend well with chocolatey or coffee flavors. Additionally, Hazelnut -infused beers are often enjoyed during colder months as they provide warmth and depth."
      },
      {
        "name": "Jasmine",
        "misc_type": "herb",
        "folder_path": "Herb",
//        "color": {
//          "unit": "EBC",
//          "value": 1.0
//        },
        "notes": "Typical color range: 0-36 EBC (typical 1)\n\nJasmine, a fragrant spice and herb, is increasingly being used in beer brewing to add unique flavors and aromas. Known for its floral profile with notes of sweet Jasmine flowers, it brings an exotic touch to the taste of beer. The delicate yet distinctive fragrance of Jasmine can greatly influence the overall flavor profile by adding a subtle sweetness and aromatic complexity.\n\nBrewers use Jasmine primarily as an adjunct ingredient to enhance specific styles or create innovative brews. It is often utilized in lighter beers such as lagers, wheat beers, or pale ales where its floral characteristics complement the crispness and lightness of these styles. Jasmine 's properties include anti-inflammatory and soothing effects on digestion, making it appealing beyond just its flavor contributions."
      },
      {
        "name": "Juniper",
        "misc_type": "spice",
        "folder_path": "Spice",
//        "color": {
//          "unit": "EBC",
//          "value": 1.0
//        },
        "notes": "Typical color range: 0-97 EBC (typical 1)\n\nJuniper is a versatile spice that has found its way into the world of beer brewing. Known for its distinct flavor profile, Juniper adds a refreshing and piney taste to beers. Its influence on the taste of beer can range from subtle undertones to bold and dominant flavors, depending on the amount used in the brewing process.\n\nIn beer brewing, Juniper is primarily used as an ingredient in specialty or seasonal brews. Its properties contribute not only to the flavor but also to the aroma and color of the final product. Juniper 's unique combination of citrusy and herbal notes lends itself well to various styles such as pale ales, saisons, stouts, and even sour beers."
      },
      {
        "name": "Lavender",
        "misc_type": "herb",
        "folder_path": "Herb",
//        "color": {
//          "unit": "EBC",
//          "value": 12.0
//        },
        "notes": "Typical color range: 5-12 EBC (typical 12)\n\nLavender, a popular spice and herb, has found its way into the world of beer brewing, adding a unique flavor profile to various styles of beer. With its distinct floral aroma and slightly sweet taste, Lavender enhances the overall sensory experience of drinking beer. Its influence on taste can vary depending on how it is used - when added during the brewing process, it imparts delicate hints of Lavender in both the aroma and flavor.\n\nApart from lending an enticing fragrance to beers, Lavender serves multiple purposes in brewing. It acts as a natural preservative due to its antimicrobial properties and can help extend the shelf life of certain brews. Additionally, this aromatic herb is often utilized for its calming effects and potential health benefits such as reducing anxiety or promoting relaxation. Beer styles that incorporate Lavender range from light Belgian witbiers to hoppy American pale ales . The use of Lavender adds complexity to these beers while complementing other ingredients like citrus fruits or spices commonly found in these styles."
      },
      {
        "name": "Mint",
        "misc_type": "herb",
        "folder_path": "Herb",
//        "color": {
//          "unit": "EBC",
//          "value": 95.0
//        },
        "notes": "Also known as: Peppermint\n\nTypical color range: 95-259 EBC (typical 95)\n\nMint is a popular herb used in beer brewing, adding a refreshing and aromatic flavor profile to the brew. Its distinctive taste of coolness and sweetness complements various beer styles, enhancing their overall taste experience. Mint influences the taste of beer by imparting its characteristic flavors through essential oils and compounds present in its leaves.\n\nIn addition to flavor enhancement, Mint is also used for its medicinal properties. It is believed to aid digestion, provide relief from nausea, and promote relaxation. These attributes make it a suitable ingredient for certain types of beers such as herbal or fruit-infused varieties.\n\nWhen using Mint in brewing, brewers can utilize fresh or dried Mint leaves during different stages of the process - including mashing, boiling, fermentation, or conditioning - depending on the desired intensity of flavor. Typical beer styles that incorporate Mint include wheat beers like Belgian Witbier or American Wheat Ale where it adds an invigorating touch to complement citrusy or fruity notes."
      },
      {
        "name": "Nutmeg",
        "misc_type": "spice",
        "folder_path": "Spice",
//        "color": {
//          "unit": "EBC",
//          "value": 1.0
//        },
        "notes": "Nutmeg is a versatile spice that finds its application in the world of beer brewing. Known for its warm and earthy flavor profile, Nutmeg adds a unique depth to the taste of beer. Its distinct sweet and slightly spicy notes create a harmonious balance with other ingredients, enhancing the overall complexity of flavors.\n\nWhen used in beer brewing, Nutmeg influences the taste by imparting a subtle yet noticeable hint of warmth and richness. It complements various styles such as stouts, porters, pumpkin beers, and winter warmers exceptionally well. Nutmeg is often added during different stages of brewing – from mashing to boiling or even during fermentation – allowing it to infuse into the beer and fully develop its aromatic properties.\n\nApart from adding flavor, Nutmeg also brings certain beneficial properties to brewed beers. It contains essential oils like myristicin which possess antimicrobial properties that can aid in preserving the freshness of the brews over time. Additionally, Nutmeg has been associated with digestive benefits when consumed in moderation.\n\nIn conclusion, Nutmeg serves as an intriguing ingredient in beer brewing due to its distinctive flavor profile and potential health benefits. Brewers creatively incorporate this spice into various styles ranging from dark winter brews to autumnal favorites like pumpkin beers for an enhanced sensory experience."
      },
      {
        "name": "Orange Peel",
        "misc_type": "other",
        "folder_path": "Other",
//        "color": {
//          "unit": "EBC",
//          "value": 1.0
//        },
        "notes": "Typical color range: 0-7 EBC (typical 1)\n\nOrange peel is a popular ingredient used in beer brewing, known for its distinct flavor profile that adds a refreshing and citrusy twist to the brew. It imparts a bright and zesty aroma with hints of sweetness and bitterness, which can greatly influence the taste of beer. The orange peel is typically added during various stages of brewing, such as during boiling or fermentation, to extract its essential oils and flavors.\n\nThe use of orange peel in beer brewing serves multiple purposes. Firstly, it acts as a natural flavor enhancer by adding complexity and depth to the overall taste profile. Additionally, it contributes acidity and brightness to balance out maltiness or hop bitterness in certain styles of beers. Orange peel also brings a pleasant visual appeal when used as garnish or added directly into the fermenter.\n\nApart from its flavorful characteristics, orange peels possess unique properties that make them suitable for brewing applications. They contain essential oils rich in limonene compounds that provide intense aromatics while their high sugar content can contribute to both alcohol production during fermentation and residual sweetness in finished beers.\n\nVarious beer styles benefit from the addition of orange peel including witbiers (Belgian-style wheat beers), Belgian tripels, saisons (farmhouse ale), pale ales, IPAs ( India Pale Ales ), fruit-infused beers like shandies or radlers, among others. In these styles, orange peel helps create an invigorating and vibrant drinking experience by imparting its delightful citric notes throughout each sip."
      },
      {
        "name": "Rooibos",
        "misc_type": "other",
        "folder_path": "Other",
//        "color": {
//          "unit": "EBC",
//          "value": 61.0
//        },
        "notes": "Typical color range: 52-70 EBC (typical 61)\n\nRooibos, a popular South African herbal tea, has found its way into the world of beer brewing and is making quite an impact. Known for its distinct flavor profile that includes sweet, nutty notes with hints of caramel and vanilla, Rooibos adds complexity and depth to beer. Its unique taste is influencing the overall flavor by providing a malty sweetness that balances out bitterness or hoppy flavors in beers.\n\nThe properties of Rooibos make it suitable for various applications in brewing. It can be used as an ingredient during mashing or steeped like tea after fermentation to infuse the desired flavors into the final product. Rooibos is often employed in specialty beers such as red ales, Belgian-style witbiers, or even stouts where it complements roasted malt characters exceptionally well.\n\nWith its versatility and flavorful qualities, Rooibos brings a touch of uniqueness to traditional beer styles while offering brewers endless possibilities for experimentation and creativity. Whether used as an infusion or added directly to the brew kettle, Rooibos provides an exciting twist on familiar beer styles that will surely entice adventurous drinkers seeking something new and delightful."
      },
      {
        "name": "Vanilla",
        "misc_type": "spice",
        "folder_path": "Spice",
//        "color": {
//          "unit": "EBC",
//          "value": 12.0
//        },
        "notes": "Typical color range: 2-47 EBC (typical 12)\n\nVanilla, a popular spice and herb, has found its way into the world of beer brewing, adding a delightful twist to various styles. Known for its sweet and creamy flavor profile with hints of caramel and floral undertones, Vanilla brings a unique richness to beers. When used in moderation, it can enhance the complexity and depth of flavors in both dark and light beers.\n\nInfluencing the taste of beer, Vanilla imparts a smooth sweetness that balances well with different malt profiles. It adds an inviting aroma that complements the overall experience of drinking beer. Vanilla is commonly used as an adjunct ingredient during brewing or added through aging on oak barrels soaked in Vanilla extract or beans. Apart from its delectable taste characteristics, Vanilla possesses certain properties that make it favorable for use in beer production. Its natural oils contribute to improved head retention while lending a velvety mouthfeel to the brews. Additionally, vanillin compounds present in Vanilla act as antioxidants which help preserve the freshness of beers over time.\n\nTypical beer styles brewed with Vanilla include stouts, porters, brown ales, cream ales, Belgian quadrupels (Quad), and barleywines. These styles benefit from the addition of Vanilla by creating harmonious flavor combinations such as chocolate- Vanilla notes in stouts or caramel- Vanilla accents in porters."
      },
      {
        "name": "Whisky",
        "misc_type": "other",
        "folder_path": "Other",
//        "color": {
//          "unit": "EBC",
//          "value": 3.0
//        },
        "notes": "Typical color range: 2-47 EBC (typical 3)\n\nWhisky, with its distinct flavor profile characterized by smoky, peaty, and caramel notes, has become a popular ingredient in beer brewing. Its influence on the taste of beer adds complexity and depth to the final product. When used in moderation, Whisky can enhance the maltiness of beers by imparting rich flavors that complement the existing ingredients.\n\nThe application of Whisky in beer brewing is versatile and can be used for various purposes. It can be added during fermentation or aging to infuse unique characteristics into the beer. Whisky also acts as a preservative due to its high alcohol content and contributes to an extended shelf life for certain styles of beers.\n\nProperties such as oakiness from barrel aging or hints of vanilla from specific whiskies lend themselves well to certain beer styles. Scotch Ales benefit from the addition of Whisky as it complements their malty sweetness perfectly. Similarly, stouts and porters gain richness when aged in whiskey barrels or infused with whiskey itself."
      },
      {
        "name": "White Pepper",
        "misc_type": "spice",
        "folder_path": "Spice",
        "notes": "White Pepper, a popular spice in Asian cuisine, has also found its way into the world of beer brewing. Known for its pungent and slightly floral flavor profile, White Pepper adds a unique twist to beers by infusing them with hints of earthiness and heat. Its influence on the taste of beer can vary depending on the amount used; a small quantity might provide subtle undertones while larger amounts can create a more pronounced spiciness.\n\nWhite Pepper is typically added during the brewing process as an ingredient to enhance complexity and depth in certain beer styles. It pairs exceptionally well with Belgian-style beers such as saisons or farmhouse ales, where it complements the fruity esters produced by Belgian yeast strains. Additionally, this spice can be incorporated into other beer styles like stouts or wheat beers to introduce an unexpected element that tantalizes the palate.\n\nApart from its distinct flavor characteristics, White Pepper possesses several properties that make it suitable for brewing applications. Firstly, it acts as a natural preservative due to its antimicrobial properties which help prolong shelf life. Furthermore, when used correctly in moderation, it contributes not only aromatic qualities but also aids in achieving balance within the overall flavor profile of the beer."
      }
    ],
